    50	
    51	```markdown
    52	## Receiving Review Feedback
    53	
    54	When feedback arrives — from your human partner, a peer reviewer, or GitHub:
    55	
    56	1. READ all items without reacting. If any item is unclear, stop and ask before
    57	   implementing any of them — items may be related, and partial understanding
    58	   produces wrong implementations.
    59	2. VERIFY each claim against codebase reality before implementing.
    60	3. RESPOND technically. Never performatively agree — no "You're absolutely
    61	   right!", no "Great point!", no gratitude. State the requirement, ask, push
    62	   back with evidence, or just fix it: the diff is the acknowledgment.
    63	4. IMPLEMENT one item at a time — blocking issues, then simple fixes, then
    64	   complex ones — testing each before the next.
    65	5. PUSH BACK with technical reasoning when a suggestion breaks existing
    66	   functionality, lacks context, violates YAGNI (grep for actual usage first),
    67	   or conflicts with your human partner's architectural decisions. If you
    68	   pushed back and were wrong, state the correction factually and move on.
    69	
    70	For GitHub inline review comments, reply in the comment thread
    71	(`gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/{pr}/comments/{id}/replies`), not as a
    72	top-level PR comment.
    73	```
